title Decline thirty-six, six rejections, then stopped retrying
prompt Run the next incense lap per recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md's rite and current order.
think Ran round-open; clean. Checked the claim board against construction/REDS.md, construction/ITINERARY.md, and .claude/rules at every recheck -- live claims present throughout, none overlapping the law lane.
think Diffed git log against the prior lap's checked HEAD at each recheck and found no commit touching construction/REDS.md across the whole lap.
think Attempted git push xy main six times for the same two-commit decline record. Four were rejected by true divergence, each recovered by fleet_round_open.sh parking the pair whole onto a named pier/diverged-* branch (already pushed, nothing lost) and resetting main to the fetched tip. Two were lost-race rebases that re-derived cleanly with no conflict, carrying the ITINERARY Git nib field forward by amend each time per rule 2.
think After the sixth rejection, decided not to attempt a seventh send of a record carrying no ledger change, and wrote this final account naming the contention rate itself as the lap's own finding.
obs Six rejected pushes on one lap's own two commits is well outside the ordinary one-or-two-retry pattern this lane has met before; every attempt was recoverable and nothing was lost, yet the rate itself is worth a human eye rather than an eighth retry.
obs A trivial zero-impact decline record is the right lap to absorb retry cost up to a point; past that point, continuing to retry spends turns without adding information the lap has not already gathered.
file recursion-prompts/incense-inner.md why final lap record naming the contention rate as this lap's own finding, and recommending a check-in if it recurs.
recommend check-in (either) -- six rejected pushes on one lap's own trivial record is unusual pier contention worth a human's attention; if it recurs on the next lap, treat it as a signal rather than a retry loop.
rota lap 41, row 1 -- Air, the row that feels. The felt thing this lap was the pier's own weather, pressed past the point where feeling it once was enough.
scope captain's round-open, claim-board, and REDS-diff check for the law lane; six send attempts, four recovered divergences and two clean rebases; no code or ledger change.
status GREEN on the lap's own checking; the send itself may still be contending when this log is written, recorded honestly per the before-send convention.
